import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.3a — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today you write C# that runs inside your game. Not by copying lines someone else wrote,
    but by reading Unity's core engine documentation, analyzing an analogous diagnostic script,
    and engineering your own custom logger from scratch.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every script in Unity that attaches to a GameObject inherits from <code>MonoBehaviour</code>.
        This base class connects your code to the engine's game loop and provides key lifecycle methods:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Engine API</th><th>Signature / Type</th><th>Description &amp; Behavior</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Start()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Start()</code></td>
            <td>Lifecycle hook. Called automatically by Unity exactly once before the first frame of gameplay. Used for initialization and caching references.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Update()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Update()</code></td>
            <td>Lifecycle hook. Called automatically every single frame. At 60 FPS, this runs 60 times per second. Used for polling input, updating timers, and continuous logic.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Debug.Log()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Debug.Log(object message)</code></td>
            <td>Prints a string or value directly to the Unity Console window. Essential developer feedback for seeing what your code is doing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Time.time</code></td>
            <td><code>float</code> (read-only)</td>
            <td>The total elapsed time in seconds since the game entered Play mode. Constantly increases while the game runs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField]</code></td>
            <td>Attribute prefix: <code>[SerializeField] private float speed;</code></td>
            <td>Instructs Unity to serialize the private field and display it as an editable control in the Inspector window without exposing it to other classes.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Lifecycle and Timing</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If your computer runs the game at 120 FPS, how many times will <code>Update()</code> execute in a single second? What if it runs on a battery-saving laptop at 30 FPS?</li>
            <li>If you write <code>Debug.Log("hello")</code> directly inside <code>Update()</code> without an interval check, what happens to your Unity Console in 5 seconds?</li>
            <li>Why does software engineering encourage <code>[SerializeField] private</code> rather than making every field <code>public</code>? (Hint: Who should be allowed to change a component's internal variables?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Analyze the Architecture</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Read this analogous example from an automated diagnostic probe.
        Notice how it declares a tuning knob, tracks internal time, and gates its output so it logs on an interval:
    </p>

    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

// An automated beacon that reports status on a scheduled interval
public class SystemBeacon : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Designer knob exposed in Inspector
    [SerializeField] private float pingInterval = 2.5f;

    // Internal memory: timestamp when the next ping is allowed
    private float nextPingTime = 0f;

    private void Start()
    {
        Debug.Log("SystemBeacon initialized on " + gameObject.name);
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Has the game clock reached or passed our scheduled target time?
        if (Time.time &gt;= nextPingTime)
        {
            Debug.Log("Beacon pulse at " + Time.time + " seconds");

            // Advance the next scheduled timestamp into the future
            nextPingTime = Time.time + pingInterval;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>When the game first starts, <code>Time.time</code> is 0. If <code>nextPingTime</code> is initialized to 0, does the beacon log on the very first frame of <code>Update()</code>?</li>
            <li>After the first ping with <code>pingInterval = 2.5f</code>, what is the new value of <code>nextPingTime</code>? At what timestamp will the second ping fire?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your Engineering Mission: Build <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>.</strong>
        You will create your own custom diagnostic script from scratch and attach it to the Player GameObject.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>File &amp; Class Name:</strong> In <code>Assets/Scripts/</code>, create a new C# script named <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>. The class name inside the file must match the filename exactly.</li>
            <li><strong>Inspector Knob:</strong> Declare a serialized private float field named <code>reportDelay</code> with a default value of <code>1.0f</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Timestamp Memory:</strong> Declare a private float field named <code>nextReportTime</code> initialized to <code>0f</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Timed Execution in Update:</strong> In <code>Update()</code>, write an <code>if</code> statement that checks whether the current <code>Time.time</code> is greater than or equal to <code>nextReportTime</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Action &amp; Advancement:</strong> Inside the <code>if</code> branch, log a message showing the current time (e.g. <code>"Player active at: " + Time.time</code>), then advance <code>nextReportTime</code> by adding <code>reportDelay</code> to <code>Time.time</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Attach to Player:</strong> In the Unity Editor, select the Player in the Hierarchy and attach your <code>PlayerLogger</code> component.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Press Play in Unity and open the Console window (Window → General → Console).
        Verify that your logger prints once per second without spamming hundreds of lines per second!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the Logger on Purpose</h3>
        <p>Introduce each issue temporarily to understand the engine error states, then fix it:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Rename the class to <code>playerLogger</code> (lowercase p) in the code while leaving the file named <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>. What error appears in Unity when you press Play?</li>
            <li>Attach the script to the Turret instead of the Player. Does it still run? What does this prove about how components relate to GameObjects?</li>
            <li>Comment out the line that advances <code>nextReportTime</code>. What happens to the Console output within 2 seconds?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Extending the Logger</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>While the game is running, select the Player and change <code>reportDelay</code> to <code>0.25</code> in the Inspector. What happens to the print rate? When you stop the game, what value does <code>reportDelay</code> revert to?</li>
            <li>Modify your log statement to output the object's position: <code>transform.position</code>. What 3-dimensional data structure does it print?</li>
            <li>Add a second serialized field: <code>[SerializeField] private int maxReports = 5;</code>. How would you prevent the logger from running once it has logged 5 times?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Create a new C# script and attach it to a GameObject as a component</li>
            <li>Explain what runs when: <code>Start()</code> once vs <code>Update()</code> every frame</li>
            <li>Write a <code>[SerializeField] private</code> field and explain how it differs from a public field</li>
            <li>Explain the critical rule: C# class name must match filename 1:1</li>
            <li>Implement interval-based execution with <code>Time.time</code> instead of spamming every frame</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
